My Zomato Frontend Interview Experience

I recently appeared for a frontend interview and here’s how the process went:

Application Process

- I applied through a referral.

- The HR reached out to me after a few days.

- The first round was scheduled, but it got rescheduled three times before it finally happened.

Round 1

Format: Technical + DSA

1. Introduction

- Brief introduction about myself and my background.

2. Project Discussion

- Detailed discussion on one of my best projects (a fintech product).
- In-depth questions around currency conversion on the frontend, handling decimal precision, and state management.

3. Core Frontend Concepts

- Comparison between Redux vs Context API and when to choose one over the other.
- Extended discussion on useState and useReducer as well.

4. DSA Problem

- I was asked to implement LRU(Least Recently Used) Cache.
- (for those who don't know what LRU is) LRU cache: A data structure that stores a fixed number of items, discarding the least recently used item when the cache is full to make room for new data.
- I solved it using objects in JavaScript and managed Dates, which worked functionally but wasn’t the most optimized approach.
- The optimal solution involves Doubly Linked List + HashMap for O(1) operations.

5. Q&A

Wrapped up with some questions from my side about their engineering practices and workflows.

6. Status: Pending/Rejected — haven’t received a response from them yet.

Key Takeaways

Preparation matters: Even as a frontend engineer, DSA questions like LRU Cache can show up.
Know your trade-offs: Solving with objects worked, but wasn’t the best approach — optimization matters.
System design & state management knowledge are crucial, especially when dealing with fintech or data-heavy applications.
Be prepared for reschedules — patience is part of the process.



